(f) Ordering rules for S corporation stock basis (IRC § 1367). For any taxable year of an S corporation, a shareholder's stock basis must be adjusted in the following strict statutory order (and never reduced below zero):
(1) Increased for all income items (both separately stated and nonseparately computed) and tax-exempt income;
(2) Decreased for non-dividend distributions made to the shareholder during the taxable year;
(3) Decreased for nondeductible, noncapital expenses (such as nondeductible fines or 50% disallowed meals) and certain oil/gas depletion deductions; and
(4) Decreased for items of deductible loss and deduction.
Because distributions reduce stock basis BEFORE deductible losses, a shareholder may receive tax-free distributions while having excess ordinary losses suspended and carried forward indefinitely until basis is restored.

(a) Ordering of C corporation distributions (IRC § 301(c) & § 316). Every corporate distribution to shareholders with respect to stock is treated in the following strict statutory hierarchy:
(1) First, as a taxable dividend to the extent of Current Earnings and Profits (CEP), allocated ratably across distributions made during the year — even if the corporation has a large negative Accumulated Earnings and Profits (AEP) deficit (the nimble dividend rule);
(2) Second, as a taxable dividend to the extent of positive Accumulated Earnings and Profits (AEP), applied chronologically;
(3) Third, as a nontaxable return of capital reducing the shareholder's adjusted stock basis down to zero; and
(4) Fourth, any remaining excess above stock basis as capital gain from the sale or exchange of property.

